  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to leverage the structured periodicity inherent in optimal Erd\u0151s minimum overlap solutions. Unlike gradient-based methods that can easily get trapped in local optima, our approach combines geometric pattern generation with multi-resolution optimization. We generate self-similar binary templates with controlled spectral content, enabling efficient exploration of the solution space. The method addresses the fundamental issue of discrete optimization in overlap integrals by embedding problem-specific structure into the search process, which gradient-based methods often fail to capture.\n\nMathematical specification\n- Representation: Piecewise constant function h(x) \u2208 {0,1}\n- Constraint: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1 (discretized as sum(h) = n_points/2)\n- Objective: min max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- We discretize the integral using linear correlation and FFT to compute overlaps across shifts\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. **Parent validation**: Verify initial_h_values satisfy sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 and 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n2. **Geometric template generation**:\n   - Create periodic templates with controlled average and autocorrelation\n   - Compute candidate solutions via spectral interpolation\n3. **Multi-scale optimization**:\n   - Local refinement with gradient-free binary search\n   - Configuration space exploration based on spectral properties\n4. **Runtime management**:\n   - Total allocation: 800s optimization, 200s cleaning/rescue\n   - Phased optimization: exploration \u2192 refinement \u2192 final polish\n5. **Fallback protocol**: If dimension changes, convert parent via linear interpolation\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n- Primary parent resolution: 380 points\n- Five-dimensional optimization space (strength/phase/width/resolution)\n- Mixture coefficients: [0.3, 0.6, 0.1] (strong/medium/weak period)\n- Three optimization phases with weighted metrics\n- Critical parameters: annealing rate=0.97, cooling speed=35 iterations\n- Step size adaptation tracking local curvature\n\nCompute budget and robustness\n- Initial population generation: O(n_points) parallel FFT operations\n- Core optimization: O(resolution) spectral computations per iteration\n- Time estimates: phase durations dynamically adjusted\n- Validation phase: projected temperature drop curve y_pred = a\u00b7exp(-b\u00b7t)\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Ensure 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n2. Verify \u222bh(x)dx = target using n_points/2 tolerance\n3. Compute C\u2085 via linear correlation normalized by dx=2.0/n_points\n4. Binary/radial evaluation: compare continuous vs binary representations\n5. Clean output: compute C\u2085 for final solution twice for verification",
